1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,076
Sweetheart, every year your mother tells me

2
00:00:03,076 --> 00:00:04,864
Christmas costs too much.

3
00:00:05,044 --> 00:00:06,653
The gifts, the lights,

4
00:00:06,653 --> 00:00:09,286
the wrapping paper you throw away in

5
00:00:09,286 --> 00:00:10,164
ten minutes.

6
00:00:10,344 --> 00:00:12,642
And every year I tell her the

7
00:00:12,642 --> 00:00:13,514
same thing.

8
00:00:13,694 --> 00:00:15,324
In nineteen fifty-one,

9
00:00:15,324 --> 00:00:18,510
my mother made the most beautiful Christmas

10
00:00:18,510 --> 00:00:19,325
I ever had,

11
00:00:19,325 --> 00:00:21,844
for four people and Grandma Pearl,

12
00:00:21,844 --> 00:00:23,771
with about twenty dollars.

13
00:00:23,951 --> 00:00:26,525
And almost every trick she used,

14
00:00:26,525 --> 00:00:28,456
you can still use today.

15
00:00:28,805 --> 00:00:30,750
So tonight I'm going to give you

16
00:00:30,750 --> 00:00:31,662
twelve of them.

17
00:00:31,842 --> 00:00:34,523
Twelve of my mother's Christmas tricks.

18
00:00:34,703 --> 00:00:36,676
I'll tell you how we did it

19
00:00:36,676 --> 00:00:38,284
in nineteen fifty-one,

20
00:00:38,284 --> 00:00:40,403
and exactly how you can do it

21
00:00:40,403 --> 00:00:42,668
this year, in your own kitchen.

22
00:00:42,848 --> 00:00:43,984
Get a pencil,

23
00:00:43,984 --> 00:00:44,508
honey.

24
00:00:44,688 --> 00:00:46,859
Your mother will want this list.

25
00:00:47,209 --> 00:00:48,475
Trick number one.

26
00:00:48,655 --> 00:00:50,187
Start in January.

27
00:00:50,367 --> 00:00:52,464
My mother kept a Mason jar on

28
00:00:52,464 --> 00:00:54,488
the top shelf of the pantry,

29
00:00:54,488 --> 00:00:57,307
and every Friday when my father brought

30
00:00:57,307 --> 00:00:58,247
home his pay,

31
00:00:58,247 --> 00:01:00,271
a quarter went into the jar.

32
00:01:00,451 --> 00:01:02,390
Sometimes fifty cents.

33
00:01:02,570 --> 00:01:04,935
By December there was enough for the

34
00:01:04,935 --> 00:01:06,183
whole of Christmas,

35
00:01:06,183 --> 00:01:07,957
and not one penny of it was

36
00:01:07,957 --> 00:01:08,549
borrowed.

37
00:01:08,729 --> 00:01:11,945
Nobody we knew bought Christmas on credit.

38
00:01:12,294 --> 00:01:15,243
Today, sweetheart, do the same thing.

39
00:01:15,423 --> 00:01:17,645
Pick a jar or a little savings

40
00:01:17,645 --> 00:01:20,237
account, and put in the same amount

41
00:01:20,237 --> 00:01:21,570
every single week,

42
00:01:21,570 --> 00:01:23,051
starting in January.

43
00:01:23,231 --> 00:01:25,573
Even five dollars a week is two

44
00:01:25,573 --> 00:01:28,444
hundred and sixty dollars by December.

45
00:01:28,624 --> 00:01:30,136
And when you shop,

46
00:01:30,136 --> 00:01:32,908
you only spend what's in the jar.

47
00:01:33,088 --> 00:01:34,259
When it's empty,

48
00:01:34,259 --> 00:01:35,724
Christmas is bought.

49
00:01:36,073 --> 00:01:37,710
Trick number two.

50
00:01:37,890 --> 00:01:39,493
Buy the tree late.

51
00:01:39,673 --> 00:01:43,004
Christmas trees in nineteen fifty-one cost about

52
00:01:43,004 --> 00:01:44,809
a dollar or two at the lot

53
00:01:44,809 --> 00:01:46,128
by the gas station.

54
00:01:46,308 --> 00:01:49,227
But on the twenty-second or twenty-third of

55
00:01:49,227 --> 00:01:51,670
December, the man wanted to go home,

56
00:01:51,670 --> 00:01:54,046
and my father would offer him fifty

57
00:01:54,046 --> 00:01:56,150
cents for the one with the bare

58
00:01:56,150 --> 00:01:56,490
side.

59
00:01:56,670 --> 00:01:58,706
You just turn the bare side to

60
00:01:58,706 --> 00:01:59,317
the wall.

61
00:01:59,497 --> 00:02:01,285
Nobody ever knows.

62
00:02:01,634 --> 00:02:03,598
My tip for a real tree,

63
00:02:03,598 --> 00:02:04,537
sweetheart.

64
00:02:04,717 --> 00:02:06,219
When you get it home,

65
00:02:06,219 --> 00:02:08,292
saw an inch off the bottom of

66
00:02:08,292 --> 00:02:09,865
the trunk, right away,

67
00:02:09,865 --> 00:02:11,796
and stand it in a bucket of

68
00:02:11,796 --> 00:02:14,656
water in the garage overnight before you

69
00:02:14,656 --> 00:02:15,514
bring it in.

70
00:02:15,694 --> 00:02:17,857
Keep it far from the radiator or

71
00:02:17,857 --> 00:02:20,358
the heater, and check the water every

72
00:02:20,358 --> 00:02:21,372
single morning.

73
00:02:21,552 --> 00:02:24,332
Our tree in nineteen fifty-one drank a

74
00:02:24,332 --> 00:02:25,210
quart a day,

75
00:02:25,210 --> 00:02:27,771
and it never dropped a needle until

76
00:02:27,771 --> 00:02:28,576
New Year's.

77
00:02:28,925 --> 00:02:30,354
Trick number three.

78
00:02:30,534 --> 00:02:33,413
Popcorn and cranberry garlands.

79
00:02:33,593 --> 00:02:35,730
My mother popped a big pot of

80
00:02:35,730 --> 00:02:38,382
popcorn on the stove the day before,

81
00:02:38,382 --> 00:02:40,298
and left it out overnight,

82
00:02:40,298 --> 00:02:43,983
because fresh popcorn crumbles but day-old popcorn

83
00:02:43,983 --> 00:02:44,425
holds.

84
00:02:44,605 --> 00:02:46,713
Then Danny and I sat at the

85
00:02:46,713 --> 00:02:49,602
kitchen table with a needle and heavy

86
00:02:49,602 --> 00:02:52,646
thread, three pieces of popcorn and one

87
00:02:52,646 --> 00:02:54,598
cranberry, over and over,

88
00:02:54,598 --> 00:02:55,379
for hours.

89
00:02:55,559 --> 00:02:57,974
And yes, we ate half of it.

90
00:02:58,323 --> 00:02:59,663
Here's my tip,

91
00:02:59,663 --> 00:03:00,715
sweetheart.

92
00:03:00,895 --> 00:03:04,304
Use upholstery thread or dental floss,

93
00:03:04,304 --> 00:03:05,829
it doesn't break.

94
00:03:06,009 --> 00:03:07,670
Wet the end of the needle.

95
00:03:07,850 --> 00:03:10,204
Make them about six feet long,

96
00:03:10,204 --> 00:03:12,715
and drape them on the tree last,

97
00:03:12,715 --> 00:03:14,049
after the lights.

98
00:03:14,229 --> 00:03:16,382
And when Christmas is over,

99
00:03:16,382 --> 00:03:19,092
take them outside and hang them on

100
00:03:19,092 --> 00:03:20,766
a bush for the birds.

101
00:03:20,946 --> 00:03:22,351
That's what we did.

102
00:03:22,531 --> 00:03:26,352
The cardinals loved Christmas almost as much

103
00:03:26,352 --> 00:03:27,221
as we did.

104
00:03:27,570 --> 00:03:29,277
Trick number four.

105
00:03:29,457 --> 00:03:30,723
Paper chains.

106
00:03:30,903 --> 00:03:33,910
Every school child in America made them.

107
00:03:34,090 --> 00:03:36,046
Strips of colored paper,

108
00:03:36,046 --> 00:03:38,736
an inch wide and six inches long,

109
00:03:38,736 --> 00:03:41,590
looped and pasted with flour paste.

110
00:03:41,770 --> 00:03:43,126
Flour and water,

111
00:03:43,126 --> 00:03:46,007
that's all paste was in our house.

112
00:03:46,187 --> 00:03:48,336
We hung them in every doorway,

113
00:03:48,336 --> 00:03:51,058
and Grandma Pearl counted the loops to

114
00:03:51,058 --> 00:03:53,780
tell us how many days until Christmas.

115
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:56,398
Tear off one loop every morning.

116
00:03:56,748 --> 00:03:59,194
Today you can make them from old

117
00:03:59,194 --> 00:04:03,014
magazines, wrapping paper scraps or the children's

118
00:04:03,014 --> 00:04:06,147
drawings, with a stapler instead of flour

119
00:04:06,147 --> 00:04:06,605
paste.

120
00:04:06,785 --> 00:04:08,840
Make one loop for every day from

121
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:10,252
the first of December,

122
00:04:10,252 --> 00:04:12,564
and write something on the inside of

123
00:04:12,564 --> 00:04:13,206
each loop.

124
00:04:13,386 --> 00:04:14,665
A Bible verse,

125
00:04:14,665 --> 00:04:16,857
a joke, or a little job.

126
00:04:17,037 --> 00:04:18,535
Call Grandma.

127
00:04:18,715 --> 00:04:20,003
Bake cookies.

128
00:04:20,183 --> 00:04:21,379
Hug your brother.

129
00:04:21,559 --> 00:04:24,744
My mother did that in nineteen fifty-three,

130
00:04:24,744 --> 00:04:26,819
and I've done it ever since.

131
00:04:27,168 --> 00:04:28,409
Trick number five,

132
00:04:28,409 --> 00:04:30,338
and this one is my favorite.

133
00:04:30,518 --> 00:04:32,051
Orange pomanders.

134
00:04:32,231 --> 00:04:34,912
You take an orange and push whole

135
00:04:34,912 --> 00:04:36,131
cloves into it,

136
00:04:36,131 --> 00:04:39,138
all over, so close they almost touch.

137
00:04:39,318 --> 00:04:41,611
My mother rolled them in cinnamon and

138
00:04:41,611 --> 00:04:43,161
set them in a dish in the

139
00:04:43,161 --> 00:04:45,578
front room, and the whole house smelled

140
00:04:45,578 --> 00:04:48,490
like Christmas from Thanksgiving to New Year's.

141
00:04:48,670 --> 00:04:51,306
No candles, no sprays.

142
00:04:51,486 --> 00:04:53,874
Just an orange and a box of

143
00:04:53,874 --> 00:04:54,493
cloves.

144
00:04:54,842 --> 00:04:55,980
My tip.

145
00:04:56,160 --> 00:04:58,410
Use a toothpick to make the holes

146
00:04:58,410 --> 00:05:00,728
first, or your thumbs will be sore

147
00:05:00,728 --> 00:05:01,478
for a week.

148
00:05:01,658 --> 00:05:03,840
Do it in early December so they

149
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:05,036
have time to dry.

150
00:05:05,216 --> 00:05:07,593
And tie a red ribbon around them

151
00:05:07,593 --> 00:05:09,524
and hang them on the tree,

152
00:05:09,524 --> 00:05:10,638
or in a closet.

153
00:05:10,818 --> 00:05:12,593
They last for years,

154
00:05:12,593 --> 00:05:13,570
sweetheart.

155
00:05:13,750 --> 00:05:15,916
I still have one my mother made

156
00:05:15,916 --> 00:05:17,175
in nineteen sixty.

157
00:05:17,355 --> 00:05:19,436
It's hard as a rock and it

158
00:05:19,436 --> 00:05:21,197
still smells like her.

159
00:05:21,547 --> 00:05:23,045
Trick number six.

160
00:05:23,225 --> 00:05:24,839
Snow on the windows.

161
00:05:25,019 --> 00:05:26,077
In the fifties,

162
00:05:26,077 --> 00:05:29,038
mothers mixed Bon Ami powder cleanser with

163
00:05:29,038 --> 00:05:31,223
a little water into a paste and

164
00:05:31,223 --> 00:05:33,409
painted it on the inside of the

165
00:05:33,409 --> 00:05:34,960
windows with a sponge,

166
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:36,652
through a paper stencil.

167
00:05:36,832 --> 00:05:38,501
A star, a bell,

168
00:05:38,501 --> 00:05:39,502
a candle.

169
00:05:39,682 --> 00:05:40,904
When it dried,

170
00:05:40,904 --> 00:05:42,736
it looked like frost.

171
00:05:42,916 --> 00:05:44,348
And after Christmas,

172
00:05:44,348 --> 00:05:46,640
you wiped it off and the windows

173
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:48,431
were cleaner than before.

174
00:05:48,611 --> 00:05:51,142
My mother liked that part best.

175
00:05:51,491 --> 00:05:53,597
Today you can do the same thing

176
00:05:53,597 --> 00:05:55,838
with a little bar soap mixed with

177
00:05:55,838 --> 00:05:58,147
water, or with the spray snow from

178
00:05:58,147 --> 00:05:59,642
the store if you like.

179
00:05:59,822 --> 00:06:02,393
Cut a stencil out of a cereal

180
00:06:02,393 --> 00:06:02,747
box.

181
00:06:02,927 --> 00:06:04,711
Do it with the children on a

182
00:06:04,711 --> 00:06:05,284
Saturday.

183
00:06:05,464 --> 00:06:07,701
The point isn't the decoration,

184
00:06:07,701 --> 00:06:08,494
sweetheart.

185
00:06:08,674 --> 00:06:10,776
The point is the Saturday.

186
00:06:11,125 --> 00:06:12,472
Trick number seven.

187
00:06:12,652 --> 00:06:14,545
Cookies are presents.

188
00:06:14,725 --> 00:06:17,363
My mother baked for two weeks before

189
00:06:17,363 --> 00:06:20,221
Christmas, and the cookies went in tins

190
00:06:20,221 --> 00:06:21,320
to the mailman,

191
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:23,152
the milkman, the teacher,

192
00:06:23,152 --> 00:06:26,230
the minister, the widow across the street.

193
00:06:26,410 --> 00:06:28,604
That was how you said thank you.

194
00:06:28,784 --> 00:06:31,337
And every lady on Elm Street did

195
00:06:31,337 --> 00:06:34,130
the same, so in the end everybody's

196
00:06:34,130 --> 00:06:37,001
tins went around the street and came

197
00:06:37,001 --> 00:06:39,953
back full of somebody else's cookies.

198
00:06:40,303 --> 00:06:42,853
Here's my mother's sugar cookie,

199
00:06:42,853 --> 00:06:44,924
sweetheart, write it down.

200
00:06:45,104 --> 00:06:47,021
One cup of butter,

201
00:06:47,021 --> 00:06:47,554
soft.

202
00:06:47,734 --> 00:06:49,394
One cup of sugar.

203
00:06:49,574 --> 00:06:50,758
One egg.

204
00:06:50,938 --> 00:06:52,993
A teaspoon of vanilla.

205
00:06:53,173 --> 00:06:55,422
Two and a half cups of flour

206
00:06:55,422 --> 00:06:57,992
and a teaspoon of baking powder.

207
00:06:58,172 --> 00:07:00,739
Mix it, wrap it in wax paper,

208
00:07:00,739 --> 00:07:03,837
and put it in the icebox overnight.

209
00:07:04,017 --> 00:07:05,261
That's the secret,

210
00:07:05,261 --> 00:07:06,780
the night in the cold.

211
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:07,863
Roll it out,

212
00:07:07,863 --> 00:07:08,992
cut your stars,

213
00:07:08,992 --> 00:07:12,003
and bake at three seventy-five for about

214
00:07:12,003 --> 00:07:15,013
eight minutes, until the edges just turn

215
00:07:15,013 --> 00:07:15,389
gold.

216
00:07:15,740 --> 00:07:17,856
And here's how you keep them,

217
00:07:17,856 --> 00:07:18,294
honey.

218
00:07:18,474 --> 00:07:21,811
Soft cookies and crisp cookies never go

219
00:07:21,811 --> 00:07:23,180
in the same tin,

220
00:07:23,180 --> 00:07:25,405
or the crisp ones go soft.

221
00:07:25,585 --> 00:07:27,823
My mother put a slice of bread

222
00:07:27,823 --> 00:07:29,987
in the tin with the soft ones

223
00:07:29,987 --> 00:07:31,404
to keep them fresh,

224
00:07:31,404 --> 00:07:33,642
and changed it every few days.

225
00:07:33,822 --> 00:07:36,094
And she always hid one tin behind

226
00:07:36,094 --> 00:07:38,642
the flour in the pantry for Christmas

227
00:07:38,642 --> 00:07:40,708
Eve, because by the twentieth,

228
00:07:40,708 --> 00:07:42,980
my father and Danny had found all

229
00:07:42,980 --> 00:07:43,737
the others.

230
00:07:44,087 --> 00:07:45,248
Trick number eight.

231
00:07:45,428 --> 00:07:48,052
Never throw away wrapping paper.

232
00:07:48,232 --> 00:07:50,831
Grandma Pearl took every piece off so

233
00:07:50,831 --> 00:07:53,360
carefully you'd think it was a bomb,

234
00:07:53,360 --> 00:07:55,467
then she ironed it flat with a

235
00:07:55,467 --> 00:07:57,575
cool iron and folded it in the

236
00:07:57,575 --> 00:07:59,401
cedar chest for next year.

237
00:07:59,581 --> 00:08:01,299
Same with the ribbons.

238
00:08:01,479 --> 00:08:03,300
Some of our paper was on its

239
00:08:03,300 --> 00:08:04,405
fourth Christmas.

240
00:08:04,585 --> 00:08:06,187
We teased her about it.

241
00:08:06,367 --> 00:08:08,353
I do it myself now.

242
00:08:08,702 --> 00:08:11,253
And when there wasn't any paper,

243
00:08:11,253 --> 00:08:13,405
we used the Sunday funnies.

244
00:08:13,585 --> 00:08:16,462
Dick Tracy and Blondie and Little Orphan

245
00:08:16,462 --> 00:08:19,051
Annie, all wrapped around a present,

246
00:08:19,051 --> 00:08:21,065
with red yarn and a sprig of

247
00:08:21,065 --> 00:08:22,431
pine from the tree.

248
00:08:22,611 --> 00:08:25,041
Or brown paper from the butcher,

249
00:08:25,041 --> 00:08:26,559
with a potato stamp.

250
00:08:26,739 --> 00:08:28,936
You cut a star into half a

251
00:08:28,936 --> 00:08:30,963
potato, dip it in paint,

252
00:08:30,963 --> 00:08:32,822
and stamp it all over.

253
00:08:33,002 --> 00:08:34,187
Children love it.

254
00:08:34,367 --> 00:08:36,561
And it costs nothing.

255
00:08:36,910 --> 00:08:38,698
Trick number nine.

256
00:08:38,878 --> 00:08:41,456
Make one gift with your own hands.

257
00:08:41,636 --> 00:08:43,880
That year I knitted my father a

258
00:08:43,880 --> 00:08:45,038
pair of mittens.

259
00:08:45,218 --> 00:08:47,354
Grandma Pearl taught me.

260
00:08:47,534 --> 00:08:49,986
One thumb was longer than the other,

261
00:08:49,986 --> 00:08:51,825
and the left one had a hole

262
00:08:51,825 --> 00:08:53,188
I didn't know about.

263
00:08:53,368 --> 00:08:56,104
He wore them every winter until nineteen

264
00:08:56,104 --> 00:08:57,472
sixty, at the plant,

265
00:08:57,472 --> 00:08:59,182
and when they fell apart,

266
00:08:59,182 --> 00:09:01,576
he kept them in his dresser drawer.

267
00:09:01,756 --> 00:09:04,020
I found them there when he died.

268
00:09:04,369 --> 00:09:06,321
Today it can be anything,

269
00:09:06,321 --> 00:09:07,179
sweetheart.

270
00:09:07,359 --> 00:09:09,654
A jar of your own cookies with

271
00:09:09,654 --> 00:09:10,343
a ribbon.

272
00:09:10,523 --> 00:09:12,822
A photograph in a frame.

273
00:09:13,002 --> 00:09:15,776
A little book of coupons for your

274
00:09:15,776 --> 00:09:18,213
mother, one breakfast in bed,

275
00:09:18,213 --> 00:09:19,811
one week of dishes,

276
00:09:19,811 --> 00:09:21,744
one hour of no arguing.

277
00:09:21,924 --> 00:09:25,141
Your great-grandfather Walter got one of those

278
00:09:25,141 --> 00:09:27,590
from Danny in nineteen fifty-three.

279
00:09:27,770 --> 00:09:29,820
He said it was the best present

280
00:09:29,820 --> 00:09:30,614
he ever got.

281
00:09:30,794 --> 00:09:33,712
He used the no-arguing coupon the same

282
00:09:33,712 --> 00:09:34,173
night.

283
00:09:34,522 --> 00:09:36,194
Trick number ten.

284
00:09:36,374 --> 00:09:38,290
An orange in the toe.

285
00:09:38,470 --> 00:09:41,082
Every stocking in our house had an

286
00:09:41,082 --> 00:09:42,849
orange down in the toe,

287
00:09:42,849 --> 00:09:45,153
and a handful of nuts in their

288
00:09:45,153 --> 00:09:47,074
shells, and a candy cane,

289
00:09:47,074 --> 00:09:48,687
and one little thing.

290
00:09:48,867 --> 00:09:50,144
A yo-yo.

291
00:09:50,324 --> 00:09:52,402
A box of crayons.

292
00:09:52,582 --> 00:09:54,149
A pair of socks.

293
00:09:54,329 --> 00:09:56,085
Grandma Pearl started it,

294
00:09:56,085 --> 00:09:58,752
because in the Depression an orange in

295
00:09:58,752 --> 00:10:00,297
December was a luxury.

296
00:10:00,477 --> 00:10:01,777
To me it still is.

297
00:10:02,127 --> 00:10:03,857
My tip, honey.

298
00:10:04,037 --> 00:10:06,714
Fill the stockings with small,

299
00:10:06,714 --> 00:10:08,588
useful, happy things.

300
00:10:08,768 --> 00:10:11,137
A toothbrush in a fun color.

301
00:10:11,317 --> 00:10:12,153
Socks.

302
00:10:12,333 --> 00:10:14,005
A little flashlight.

303
00:10:14,185 --> 00:10:15,439
Some chocolate.

304
00:10:15,619 --> 00:10:17,627
And always the orange.

305
00:10:17,807 --> 00:10:20,308
It's the cheapest thing in the stocking,

306
00:10:20,308 --> 00:10:22,497
and it's the one your children will

307
00:10:22,497 --> 00:10:24,123
remember when they're old.

308
00:10:24,472 --> 00:10:26,121
Trick number eleven.

309
00:10:26,301 --> 00:10:27,926
Music is free.

310
00:10:28,106 --> 00:10:29,894
In nineteen fifty-one,

311
00:10:29,894 --> 00:10:33,633
the radio played Christmas songs all December.

312
00:10:33,813 --> 00:10:38,035
Gene Autry singing Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer,

313
00:10:38,035 --> 00:10:40,146
which was brand new then,

314
00:10:40,146 --> 00:10:42,088
and Frosty the Snowman,

315
00:10:42,088 --> 00:10:43,101
newer still.

316
00:10:43,281 --> 00:10:44,805
And Bing Crosby,

317
00:10:44,805 --> 00:10:47,376
of course, White Christmas,

318
00:10:47,376 --> 00:10:48,424
every hour.

319
00:10:48,604 --> 00:10:50,923
We had no money for records,

320
00:10:50,923 --> 00:10:53,822
so we sang around the upright piano

321
00:10:53,822 --> 00:10:55,396
at Grandma Pearl's.

322
00:10:55,576 --> 00:10:57,371
Every night of Advent,

323
00:10:57,371 --> 00:10:58,432
after supper.

324
00:10:58,781 --> 00:11:00,848
Do that, sweetheart.

325
00:11:01,028 --> 00:11:03,749
One night, turn off every screen in

326
00:11:03,749 --> 00:11:06,238
the house, put on the old songs,

327
00:11:06,238 --> 00:11:06,938
and sing.

328
00:11:07,118 --> 00:11:09,486
Badly, it doesn't matter.

329
00:11:09,666 --> 00:11:12,513
Our Danny sang so off-key that the

330
00:11:12,513 --> 00:11:14,020
dog left the room.

331
00:11:14,200 --> 00:11:16,700
That's the memory I have of Christmas,

332
00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:18,147
more than any present.

333
00:11:18,327 --> 00:11:21,231
Danny singing, and the dog leaving the

334
00:11:21,231 --> 00:11:21,613
room.

335
00:11:21,962 --> 00:11:24,049
And trick number twelve,

336
00:11:24,049 --> 00:11:26,049
the most important one.

337
00:11:26,229 --> 00:11:29,224
Every Christmas, my mother picked one family

338
00:11:29,224 --> 00:11:31,334
at church who was having a hard

339
00:11:31,334 --> 00:11:31,674
year.

340
00:11:31,854 --> 00:11:33,572
A father out of work,

341
00:11:33,572 --> 00:11:35,372
a mother who was sick.

342
00:11:35,552 --> 00:11:37,449
And without telling anyone,

343
00:11:37,449 --> 00:11:39,698
she'd leave a box on their porch

344
00:11:39,698 --> 00:11:40,893
on Christmas Eve.

345
00:11:41,073 --> 00:11:42,513
A chicken, a pie,

346
00:11:42,513 --> 00:11:46,158
some oranges, and something for each child.

347
00:11:46,338 --> 00:11:47,731
We never signed it.

348
00:11:47,911 --> 00:11:49,639
She said if you sign it,

349
00:11:49,639 --> 00:11:50,790
it's not a gift.

350
00:11:51,140 --> 00:11:53,375
Danny and I were the ones who

351
00:11:53,375 --> 00:11:55,378
ran it up to the porch and

352
00:11:55,378 --> 00:11:57,073
rang the bell and ran.

353
00:11:57,253 --> 00:11:59,563
We hid behind the hedge to watch

354
00:11:59,563 --> 00:12:00,574
the door open.

355
00:12:00,754 --> 00:12:03,237
That was the best part of Christmas

356
00:12:03,237 --> 00:12:04,585
for me, sweetheart.

357
00:12:04,765 --> 00:12:07,157
Better than any present I ever got.

358
00:12:07,337 --> 00:12:09,357
And you can do it this year.

359
00:12:09,537 --> 00:12:12,102
It doesn't take twenty dollars.

360
00:12:12,282 --> 00:12:14,570
It just takes one porch.

361
00:12:14,919 --> 00:12:16,069
So there they are.

362
00:12:16,249 --> 00:12:19,082
Twelve tricks from nineteen fifty-one.

363
00:12:19,262 --> 00:12:21,178
The jar, the late tree,

364
00:12:21,178 --> 00:12:23,678
the popcorn, the paper chains,

365
00:12:23,678 --> 00:12:26,261
the pomanders, the window snow,

366
00:12:26,261 --> 00:12:28,428
the cookies, the wrapping,

367
00:12:28,428 --> 00:12:29,927
the homemade gift,

368
00:12:29,927 --> 00:12:31,761
the orange in the toe,

369
00:12:31,761 --> 00:12:34,760
the music, and the box on somebody's

370
00:12:34,760 --> 00:12:35,260
porch.

371
00:12:35,440 --> 00:12:38,861
Tomorrow night I'll tell you another Christmas,

372
00:12:38,861 --> 00:12:40,026
and another tip.

373
00:12:40,206 --> 00:12:42,295
Every day until Christmas,

374
00:12:42,295 --> 00:12:43,178
sweetheart.

375
00:12:43,528 --> 00:12:45,421
And tell me in the comments.

376
00:12:45,601 --> 00:12:47,864
What was the one thing your mother

377
00:12:47,864 --> 00:12:50,393
always did at Christmas that you still

378
00:12:50,393 --> 00:12:50,593
do?

379
00:12:50,773 --> 00:12:52,851
I'll read every single one.

380
00:12:53,031 --> 00:12:55,040
Goodnight, sweetheart.

381
00:12:55,220 --> 00:12:58,401
Grandma Ruth will be right here tomorrow.
